    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        GitInject: Real-World Prompt Injection Attacks in AI-Powered CI/CD Pipelines
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Isbarov%2C+J">Jafar Isbarov</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Suleymanov%2C+U">Umid Suleymanov</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Shumailov%2C+I">Ilia Shumailov</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Kantarcioglu%2C+M">Murat Kantarcioglu</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2606.09935v2-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        AI <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> are increasingly embedded in continuous integration and continuous delivery (CI/CD) workflows to autonomously review pull requests, triage issues, and maintain codebases. We study fully automated configurations in which external contributions invoke&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2606.09935v2-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2606.09935v2-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2606.09935v2-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        AI <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> are increasingly embedded in continuous integration and continuous delivery (CI/CD) workflows to autonomously review pull requests, triage issues, and maintain codebases. We study fully automated configurations in which external contributions invoke <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> without prior human <span class="search-hit mathjax">approval</span>. Because these <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> ingest untrusted content while operating with repository credentials and tools, prompt injection can have software supply chain consequences. We present GitInject, an open-source framework for evaluating prompt injection in live GitHub Actions workflows. Unlike benchmarks that simulate tool calls, GitInject provisions ephemeral repositories and executes actual workflows, exposing platform semantics such as sandbox constraints, credential handling, and permission boundaries. We use GitInject to evaluate eleven named attack scenarios across five workflows from four AI providers, covering configuration-file injection, credential exfiltration, judgment <span class="search-hit mathjax">manipulation</span>, and availability. Every provider is susceptible to at least one high-severity attack in the tested configurations. The most consequential vulnerabilities are structural: attacker-controlled configuration files from an unmerged contribution are loaded as operator-level instructions, allowing the <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>--infrastructure interaction to expose credentials or corrupt review judgments. Two configuration-file attacks replicate on GitLab and Hugging Face as well as GitHub. We evaluate workflow-level countermeasures and show their coverage and utility tradeoffs. We release GitInject and all attack scenarios.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2606.09935v2-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2606.09935v2-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 2 November, 2026; <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">v1</span> submitted 7 June, 2026;
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> June 2026.
      
    </p>
    

    

    
